From e07d018c19ca0b682d17ff0220ccc93713cb9f88 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 12:56:21 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 1/4] CUDA: dedupe q8_1 re-quantization of shared mmvq
 activations

Port of the R9700/HIP track patch 0003 ("CUDA/HIP: dedupe q8_1
re-quantization of shared mmvq activations", Claude Fable 5) to the GB10
CUDA track, with one CUDA-specific change described below. The dedupe
itself is unchanged: ggml_cuda_mul_mat_vec_q re-runs quantize_q8_1 on the
same activation once per consuming op (attention Q/K/V take the same
normed hidden state; the MoE expert matvecs take a reshaped view of the
same tensor as the shared expert), so a per-graph cache keyed on
(view-chain root, data pointer, stream, full quantize parameter tuple)
computes each distinct quantization once. A hit returns exactly the bytes
the skipped kernel would have written, so output is bit-identical by
construction; the mmvq kernels themselves are untouched.

CUDA-specific change: buffer lifetime must respect the VMM pool.

ggml_backend_cuda_context::pool() returns ggml_cuda_pool_vmm whenever the
device reports virtual-memory support, which is the case on GB10 (and on
NVIDIA generally); the HIP build falls back to ggml_cuda_pool_leg. The VMM
pool is a strict stack - ggml_cuda_pool_vmm::free asserts that the pointer
being freed is the most recent allocation - while the leg pool is a
free-list that accepts frees in any order. A cache entry outlives the op
that created it by design, so the upstream (HIP) form of this patch, which
gives every entry its own ggml_cuda_pool_alloc, aborts on CUDA the first
time an entry is evicted or an evaluation ends with an allocation on top:

  ggml-cuda.cu:681: GGML_ASSERT(ptr == (char *)pool_addr + pool_used) failed
  #4 std::vector<std::unique_ptr<ggml_cuda_quantized_src1_entry>>::_M_erase
  #5 ggml_cuda_mul_mat_vec_q

The cached buffers therefore live in a single slab allocated on entry to
ggml_backend_cuda_graph_compute - before any op has allocated - and
released on exit, after every op has freed. Being the bottom of the stack,
that allocation is always legal, under either pool. Entries bump-allocate
128-byte-aligned slices of the slab; an entry dropped by invalidation or
by the 16-entry lookup cap simply stops being findable, and no free
happens mid-evaluation. If a request does not fit, insert() returns
nullptr and the caller quantizes into its own scoped allocation exactly as
stock does - a miss is never a correctness difference - and the slab grows
for the next evaluation (1 MiB initially, capped at 64 MiB).

Verified on DGX Spark GB10 (sm_121, CUDA 13.0) against the stock build of
the same commit: greedy text and full float top-5 logprobs hash-match on
3 prompts, after a stock-vs-stock control (same process and fresh boot).
